  "story": {
    "title": "Before it was Sherwood Park",
    "source": "Strathcona County History",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "In the mid-1950s, two developers, John Campbell and John Mitchell, had a modest idea: a little satellite town of about 100 houses at the edge of the prairie, for families working at the plants east of Edmonton."
      },
      {
        "type": "statement",
        "text": "They called it Campbelltown.",
        "em": "Campbelltown"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "In December 1955, the first family moved into a model home on Birch Avenue. Houses sold for $10,459 to $14,100, and neighbours knew each other by name."
      },
      {
        "type": "streets",
        "items": [
          "Cottonwood",
          "Conifer",
          "Birch",
          "Alder",
          "Fir"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "There was just one problem. Canada Post wouldn't accept the name, because too many places were called something similar. One early resident even had a cheque from Edmonton arrive by way of Campbeltown, Scotland.",
        "em": "Campbeltown, Scotland"
      },
      {
        "type": "statement",
        "text": "So in 1956, Campbelltown became Sherwood Park.",
        "em": "Sherwood Park"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "The houses got bigger and the streets kept going, but the idea never changed: a place where neighbours meet at the end of the day."
      },
      {
        "type": "closing",
        "text": "Pull up a chair. You're among neighbours."
      }
    ]
  },
